title Nine printers become one, and the breach gets a guard
prompt kg -- after the %374 grant landed and the send went out, continue with the charter's step 4.
think The charter's steps 1 to 3 shipped at 5bd1a43e71 with the hot pass GREEN over that exact tree, so step 4 opened on a clean tree and its own lap.
think The first draft of the launcher looped over seats in Rishi and was wrong. Rishi's for-each takes one statement over a list LITERAL and the language has no string split, so a launcher looping over seats would have to spell the seat list again -- the seventh copy the whole table exists to prevent. The per-seat lines moved into the reader's --recipe mode, which is the tree's own division of labor: fixtures do the text work, the .rish asserts and says.
think The syntax was proven before the file touched the tree by running it from the scratchpad against the repo root. It parsed, reached the recipe step, and refused precisely because the staged reader had no --recipe yet -- which is the answer that tells you both halves are right.
obs A latent fault caught before it landed: --recipe is the one branch that calls itself, so a pen pointing FLEET_ROSTER at its own table would have silently read the tree's roster instead. That is the quietest kind of wrong answer because it looks exactly like a right one. The pin is exported now and a leg proves a pen roster drives the recipe.
obs The breach is enforced rather than declared. Six elder printers carry an astrological modality in a living filename -- planet, fixed, cardinal, dual -- and each keeps every byte it wrote, bannered, Class H, cut RED until circled. What a guard can do is catch the NEXT one: a living launcher filename carrying a modality word and NOT carrying the elder banner reds at zero, proven from both sides in a pen. A declaration nothing measures is a habit.
obs The ship names are untouched. Sound, Mind, Mystery, Silence, Hush and Dream pass the Comlink tendency's three tests, collide with nothing, and three may sail again. What retires is the modality word in a living filename, never the ship that wore it.
obs The captain's own seat prompt named the pier's clone ~/grain and there is no ~/grain on Dallas -- the elder is dated testimony and keeps its words, so the correction rides a fresh-stamped mutant with the elder bannered and Class H'd. It also carries the two costs a fresh sitting needs: a full cold pass runs past thirty minutes here, and a round that adds a witness must regenerate two pages BEFORE the hot pass, since the hook refreshes them at commit and the pass reads them earlier.
obs The card took eleven more condensations to fit this lap and closed at 24,575 of 24,576 bytes. One byte of headroom is not a margin, it is a coincidence. Second cairn of the session, and the pair is the measurement rather than the complaint.
loom witness=fleet_roster legs=28 new_legs=11 wall_s=4
loom roster=standing_equipment hot=1 guards=111 green=106 red=1 gated=4 wall_s=1614 tree_moved=no
obs The hot pass closed with one red and it was prose, not code: shell_dialect counts readlink -f sites across the whole tree including documents, and this lap's new launcher explained in a say line why agent-jail.sh is Linux-only. Eight sites against a ceiling of seven, and a ceiling only falls. The sentence says the same thing without the flag now, and the guard reads seven. Worth recording because the meter reaches further than a reader expects: a dialect ceiling is not only about what a script RUNS, it is about what the tree SAYS, and one honest sentence of documentation spent the last slot.
obs That one-word repair means the full pass measured a tree differing from the commit by a single say line. Rather than spend twenty-seven minutes reproving 110 unchanged readings, the changed guard was re-run by name and closed green with run_verdict=ok, and the launcher's own control re-ran at 28 of 28. Named here rather than glossed: the full green covers the tree minus that line, and the line's own guard is green beside it.
loom card_bytes=24575 card_bound=24576 cairns_this_session=2
file tools/l/launch-fleet-chapter.rish one printer, no seat in its filename, reading the roster
file tools/fixtures/f/fleet_roster_scan.sh a --recipe mode, with the roster pin exported through the one branch that recurses
file tools/fixtures/f/fleet_roster_control.sh eleven more legs, including the breach guard proven from both sides in a pen
file construction/SHRED_PREP.md six Class H rows; the cut stays RED until circled
file expanding-prompts/20260904-193221_incense-the-field-captain-two-doors.md the captain prompt molted -- the pier's tree has a name, and the fleet has a roster
obs A third pin at its ceiling, found by a guard rather than by care. Seven Class H rows pushed construction/SHRED_PREP.md from 24,366 to 25,918 against a 24,576 bound, and equinox_e123_living_pin_guard caught it on the hot pass -- the pin had 210 bytes of headroom before this lap touched it. The completed second mitra shed's record folded whole to construction/archive/, links re-anchored, which is ORGANIZING's own definition of archive rather than a convenience.
obs Three living pins at their ceilings in one session is the finding, and it is measured rather than felt: the card twice at one and 23 bytes spare, the shred-prep pin at 210 before the lap and 144 after the fold. The near-bound reading ADVISES; a guard one layer over REFUSES. So a pin arrives at its ceiling as a refusal in the middle of a round rather than as a warning a lap earlier, which is a door worth Keaton's word.
file construction/SHRED_PREP.md seven Class H rows, and the completed shed's record folded to the archive to make room for them
file construction/archive/SHRED_PREP-second-mitra-shed-record.md the second mitra shed's ten rows, byte for byte, links re-anchored one directory deeper
file construction/CHECKPOINTS.md three cairns this session, each naming what a condensation swept and why
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- three pins sit at their ceilings; raise a bound, fold a section, or let the near-bound reading gate rather than advise, so a pin warns a lap before it refuses
